"The diagnostic is GCC telling you exactly where and why parsing failed:\n\n**1. Location context — `broken.c:3:30`**\n- `3` = line number, `30` = column. Column 30 is the position immediately *after* the closing `)` of the `printf` call (the line is 29 characters long, so the caret sits just past its end — where the parser was looking for the next token).\n\n**2. Scope context — `In function 'main':`**\n- Errors are reported relative to the enclosing definition, so you know the problem is inside `main`, not some other function.\n\n**3. The core message — `error: expected ';' before 'return'`**\n- In C, an expression statement like `printf(\"Hello, World!\\n\")` must be terminated by a semicolon (C11 §6.8.3: the grammar is `expression-statement: expression_opt ;`).\n- The parser had consumed the full `printf(...)` call and then read the next token, which was the `return` keyword from line 4. Since the grammar demands `;` at that point, GCC reports the mismatch as \"expected `;` before `return`\" — naming the token it actually found (`return`).\n\n**4. The annotations**\n- The `^` at column 30 marks the insertion point where the `;` belongs; the `;` printed beneath it is GCC suggesting the fix.\n- The `~~~~~~` underline under `return` in the line-4 excerpt highlights the offending token — the one the parser encountered instead of the expected semicolon.\n\n**5. Severity and consequence**\n- The `error:` prefix (vs. `warning:`) means this is a fatal syntax error: GCC stopped before code generation and linking, produced no `broken` binary, and exited with status 1. Note that `-Wall` is irrelevant here — it only enables extra warnings, and this error occurs with any flags.\n- The `\\n` visible inside the string in the excerpt is just the escape sequence in the source text; it has nothing to do with the error.\n\n**Fix:** add a semicolon at the end of broken.c:3 — `printf(\"Hello, World!\\n\");` — and the file will compile cleanly with no warnings."